## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement a compact ranking structure with duplicates and a fixed value range, using O(log U) updates and O(log U) kth-order queries.

## Key Concepts

- Fenwick tree for prefix sums
- Binary lifting to find k-th order statistic (standard BIT trick: find largest power of 2 <= n, then iterate downward checking cumulative tree values)
- Handling duplicates via per-value counts

## Code Example

```javascript
class Fenwick {
  constructor(n) { 
    this.n = n; 
    this.tree = new Array(n + 1).fill(0); 
  }

  add(i, delta) {
    for (; i <= this.n; i += i & -i) this.tree[i] += delta;
  }

  sum(i) {
    let s = 0;
    for (; i > 0; i -= i & -i) s += this.tree[i];
    return s;
  }

  kth(k) {
    if (k < 1) return -1;
    const total = this.sum(this.n);
    if (k > total) return -1;

    let idx = 0;
    let bit = 1;
    while (bit << 1 <= this.n) bit <<= 1;

    let sum = 0;
    for (; bit > 0; bit >>= 1) {
      const next = idx + bit;
      if (next <= this.n && sum + this.tree[next] < k) {
        sum += this.tree[next];
        idx = next;
      }
    }
    return idx; // 0-indexed
  }
}

class Leaderboard {
  constructor(maxScore = 1000000) {
    this.bit = new Fenwick(maxScore + 1);
  }

  insertScore(s) { this.bit.add(s + 1, 1); }

  eraseScore(s) { this.bit.add(s + 1, -1); }

  getKthScore(k) { return this.bit.kth(k) - 1; }

  getRank(s) { return this.bit.sum(s + 1); }
}
```

## Handling Ties

Since each score has its own count, duplicates naturally work:
- insertScore(50) adds count at index 50
- insertScore(50) again increments same index
- eraseScore(50) decrements it
- getRank(50) returns all scores <= 50 including duplicates
- getKthScore(k) skips to the k-th element accounting for counts

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical string data-struct understanding: duplicates, memory use, and prefix-based queries via a Trie vs simpler maps.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node passCount and endCount
- Duplicate handling via endCount; memory managed by lazy pruning
- Time complexity O(|word|) insert/delete and O(|prefix|) query

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.pass = 0;
    this.end = 0;
  }
}

class WordDict {
  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }
  insert(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    node.pass++;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) node.children.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      node = node.children.get(ch);
      node.pass++;
    }
    node.end++;
  }
  delete(word) {
    if (!this._exists(word)) return false;
    let node = this.root;
    node.pass--;
    for (const ch of word) {
      const next = node.children.get(ch);
      next.pass--;
      if (next.pass === 0) { node.children.delete(ch); return true; }
      node = next;
    }
    node.end--;
    return true;
  }
  countPrefix(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) return 0;
      node = node.children.get(ch);
    }
    return node.pass;
  }
  _exists(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) return false;
      node = node.children.get(ch);
    }
    return node.end > 0;
  }
}
```

